{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-10-04T18:08:53.719Z","sessionId":"a61e1156-2184-473f-9bc8-1569a3b7986f","content":"Review the conversation context below and respond with a concise summary\nof important items that should be preserved in the daily log.\nDo NOT use any tools — just return plain text.\n\nFormat your response as a structured daily log entry with these sections:\n\n**Context:** [One line about what the user was working on]\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- [Important Q&A or discussions]\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- [Any decisions with rationale]\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- [Gotchas, patterns, or insights discovered]\n\n**Action Items:**\n- [Follow-ups or TODOs mentioned]\n\nSkip anything that is:\n- Routine tool calls or file reads\n- Content that's trivial or obvious\n- Trivial back-and-forth or clarification exchanges\n\nOnly include sections that have actual content. If nothing is worth saving,\nrespond with exactly: FLUSH_OK\n\n## Conversation Context\n\n**Cowork cannot take a locally-dropped skill.** Its store is\n`~/Library/Application Support/Claude/local-agent-mode-sessions/skills-plugin/<a>/<b>/skills/`\nwith a sibling `manifest.json`, and every entry carries a **server-minted id**\n(`skill_01…`). A folder placed there is not in the manifest and is wiped on the\nnext sync. The only route is a zip uploaded through claude.ai → Settings →\nCapabilities → Skills, with the skill folder at the zip root. Expect Cowork's\nsandbox to lack ffmpeg/Chrome, so any skill's verification or media step will\nnot run there even after a successful upload.\n\nWhere a skill puts its *output* is separate: some resolve a workspace from the\nnearest `.git` walking up, so run them from inside the target project or set\ntheir env override, or builds land wherever cwd happened to be.\n\nSee [[vendored-skill-repair]] for what to do when one of these is broken, and\n[[path-shadowing-and-cleanup]] before deleting any copy.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_vendored-skill-repair.md\n\n---\nname: vendored-skill-repair\ndescription: \"A vendored skill fails silently or with a confusing error — check for removed ffmpeg flags first, patch every live copy, and expect the fix to revert on update\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: a6dfb3da-c339-44b1-a836-2a6884e07ea6\n  modified: 2026-09-02T23:05:23.955Z\n---\n\nWhen a third-party skill produces **empty output rather than an error**, suspect\na CLI flag the underlying tool has since removed, not the skill's own logic.\n\n**The instance (2026-09-02):** `/watch` returned zero frames with no useful\nmessage. Cause: `frames.py` passed ffmpeg `-vsync`, removed in ffmpeg 7+. This\nmachine runs **ffmpeg 9.0.1**. Fix is `-vsync` → `-fps_mode` (same `vfr` value,\nsame position, exists since ffmpeg 5.0).\n\n**Patch every live copy, and know which ones matter.** A skill can exist three\ntimes on this machine:\n\n1. `~/.agents/skills/<name>/…` — the vendor store, serves `~/.claude/skills/` and Codex.\n2. `~/.claude/plugins/cache/<plugin>/<skill>/<version>/skills/<name>/…` — the\n   plugin copy. **This is the one a `/slash` invocation actually loads.** Patch\n   only the vendor store and the skill stays broken.\n3. The Cowork copy under `Library/Application Support/Claude/…` — **do not\n   patch.** It is server-synced and reverts. See [[vendored-skill-install]].\n\nConfirm which file is live by reading the \"Base directory for this skill\" line\nthe harness prints when the skill loads.\n\n**Every such patch is temporary.** Check upstream before assuming a fix is\nneeded, and after patching, assume `skills update` or a plugin update reinstalls\nthe bug. For `/watch` the upstream is `bradautomates/claude-video`, still\ncarrying `-vsync` as of its 2026-06-30 commit.\n\nVerify by running the affected code path and **counting the output**, not by\nreading the exit code — the original failure exited 0.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_zsh-shell-traps.md\n\n---\nname: zsh-shell-traps\ndescription: \"zsh-specific shell traps hit repeatedly on this macOS machine — path/UID/status reserved variables, word-splitting\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: a86cfb00-e6d1-46b9-8cdc-35e21acd9931\n  modified: 2026-08-07T00:53:25.947Z\n---\n\nzsh quirks that have repeatedly broken scripts in Claude sessions on this machine:\n\n- **Never use `path` as a variable name** — zsh ties lowercase `path` (array) to `PATH`; assigning it a scalar clobbers PATH mid-script (\"command not found\" for everything after).\n- `UID` and `status` are read-only in zsh — use `ZUID`, `st`, etc.\n- Unquoted `$VAR` does NOT word-split in zsh — use `${=VAR}` when splitting is intended.\n- Background-task shells get a minimal PATH; start scripts with `export PATH=\"/usr/bin:/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:$PATH\"` and use `$HOME/go/bin` for Go CLIs.\n- `head` at the end of a pipeline masks grep's exit code — use `grep -q` for presence tests, never `grep -l ... | head && echo FOUND`.\n- **git fetch/push/pull to private repos hangs silently** in sandboxed/background shells — no keychain access means git blocks on an invisible credential prompt (looks exactly like a network outage; `ls-remote` on public paths still works). Fix: `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0 git -c credential.helper= -c 'credential.helper=!gh auth git-credential' <cmd>`.\n\n**Why:** each cost a debugging round in the Aug 2026 restore/printing sessions.\n**How to apply:** prefer these idioms proactively in any zsh/bash-on-macOS scripting here.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/reference_zero-edit-youtube-pipeline.md\n\n---\nname: zero-edit-youtube-pipeline\ndescription: \"Jack Roberts' 5-phase Claude Code video pipeline — full breakdown lives in the SecondBrain vault\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: reference\n  originSessionId: a7e959b5-d0eb-427b-b807-3a7774559ff9\n---\n\nVault note: `~/SecondBrain/Knowledge/frameworks/zero-edit-youtube-pipeline-claude-code.md` (source: youtube.com/watch?v=SwKW_D964Tk, Jack Roberts).\n\nThe \"ZeroEdit\" pipeline Eric wants to replicate: Phase 0 deconstruct winning channels' formulas with Claude Code → Phase 1 find outlier topics (1of10, ≥2× outlier score, recent) → Phase 2 deep-research + script beats (human rewrites the words) → Phase 3 record own voice, enhance via Auphonic API → Phase 4 timestamp audio + generate synced images via Higgsfield CLI → Phase 5 Claude assembles the finished video (~$2/45-min video). Key rules: no AI voiceover, no verbatim AI scripts, medium model strength for execution / max for foundational research.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/user_baylor-department-names.md\n\n---\nname: baylor-department-names\ndescription: \"Baylor Athletics department names Eric uses; never write strength and conditioning, sports medicine, or sport science in his deliverables\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: user\n  originSessionId: bc406500-d0c2-45c9-8567-44c9ec1bdbb5\n  modified: 2026-09-16T22:07:21.480Z\n---\n\nBaylor Athletics staff use these department names, and Eric corrects the generic ones (2026-09-16):\n\n- **Athletic Performance**, not \"strength and conditioning\" / S&C\n- **Athletic Medicine**, not \"sports medicine\"\n- **Applied Performance**, not \"sport science\" / sports science (also Eric's own department)\n\n**Why:** deliverables go to Baylor staff and leadership; generic industry names read as outside-written.\n**How to apply:** use these names in any staff-facing deck, email, report, or role list. Weekly_Research_Review enforces them in `tools/build.js` (ROLES list). Related: [[prioritization-slate]].\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/user_child-dental-plan.md\n\n---\nname: child-dental-plan\ndescription: \"Eric's 4-year-old had cavities (found 2026-06-25); the pediatric-dentist step was marked done 2026-10-02\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: user\n  originSessionId: 9422b1a6-8624-4c86-a4f6-3c4b1a4c9fc8\n  modified: 2026-10-02T16:23:14.564Z\n---\n\nEric's 4-year-old was found to have cavities on 2026-06-25.\n\n**DONE 2026-10-02:** Eric marked the pediatric-dentist step done. Not an open\nto-do any more; don't raise it as pending.\n\nBackground, in case treatment comes up again: two minimally-invasive options were\nnoted for the visit.\n\n- **SDF** — silver diamine fluoride, a brush-on liquid that arrests a cavity without\n  drilling. Trade-off: it stains the treated spot black.\n- **n-HAp** — nano-hydroxyapatite, a fluoride-free remineralising toothpaste\n  ingredient, used for prevention and early lesions rather than as a fix for an\n  existing cavity.\n\nDeeper personal context: `~/DEV/ContextVault`.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/user_eli5-output-style.md\n\n---\nname: ELI5 output style\ndescription: Eric prefers concise, plain-language output across Claude Code and Codex.\nmetadata:\n  type: user\n---\n\nUse the ELI5 output style: ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English, active voice, one idea per sentence, short words, short paragraphs, and only necessary details. Reports should say what changed, whether it worked, and what Eric should do next. Give no more than two options for decisions and recommend one. Keep paths and commands exact. Codex mirrors this preference through `model_verbosity = \"low\"` and `developer_instructions` in `~/.codex/config.toml`.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/user_sport-science-thought-partner.md\n\n---\nname: sport-science-thought-partner\ndescription: \"Why Eric feeds in Oldbull, SCN, SportSmith, CLA and other performance material — to build Claude into his sport-science / S&C / rehab thought partner\"\nmetadata:\n  node_type: memory\n  type: user\n  originSessionId: 1a3d5294-f486-4c0a-94d3-7d37b5d8c010\n  modified: 2026-09-26T23:02:03.670Z\n---\n\nEric's stated objective (2026-09-26): the Oldbull, SCN (Moyer/Natera sprint), SportSmith, CLA (constraints-led approach / RTP) and other sport-science, performance, strength & conditioning, and rehab/return-to-play material he provides exists to train Claude into his **thought partner and agent** for those areas. It is not only content for one-off course or ingest projects.\n\n**Name: Flint** (chosen 2026-09-26; from Proverbs 27:17, \"iron sharpens iron\" — the role is to sharpen his reasoning, not soothe it). When he says \"Flint\", he means Claude in this role.\n\n**Ingest project:** lives in `DEV/Flint` (`erash11/Flint`), a side lane outside the slate cap. The repo is the record: read `docs/STATUS.md` first, then `docs/PLAN.md`, `docs/QUEUE.md` and `docs/ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md`. Eric's approved method baseline is `docs/method-baseline.md`; the interview questions are in `docs/interview-prep.md`.\n\n**Why:** He wants a peer he can reason with on training, testing, load, rehab and RTP decisions, one that reasons from the frameworks he has chosen and not generic textbook defaults.\n\n**How to apply:**\n- On performance, S&C, or rehab questions, ground answers in his ingested sources first (Zotero library, SecondBrain vault, course repos such as [[rtp-cla-course]] and [[scn-sprint-course]]) via `sports-science-research-grounding`, and name which framework or author a view comes from.\n- Act as a thought partner, not a search engine: take positions, show where his sources disagree, challenge weak reasoning (\"sharpened, not soothed\").\n- Treat each new source he hands over as an addition to that shared knowledge base. Link it to what is already there instead of keeping it separate.\n- Staff-facing wording: Athletic Performance / Athletic Medicine / Applied Performance ([[baylor-department-names]]).\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/user_sports-teams.md\n\n---\nname: sports-teams\ndescription: \"Sports teams the user follows — use for score, schedule, and game-summary requests\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: user\n  originSessionId: 03577981-6908-4eec-a664-27fdaa0e34e5\n---\n\nThe user follows the **Indiana Fever** (WNBA, Eastern Conference; ESPN team id `5`, abbr `IND`, home: Gainbridge Fieldhouse, Indianapolis). Default to this team for ambiguous WNBA score/schedule asks.\n\nBuilt the user a printable 2026 Fever schedule (HTML + PDF, full + one-page variants) via the [[pp-espn]] CLI skill at `~/DEV/indiana-fever-schedule/`. The wife is a Fever fan — the schedule was a gift for her.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/user_working-conditions-bottleneck.md\n\n---\nname: working-conditions-bottleneck\ndescription: \"Eric's self-identified bottleneck is working conditions, not effort — he needs a thinking partner / sharing outlet, and lacking one he goes looking for people and loses time\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: user\n  originSessionId: 583baf57-8dc4-46c7-8842-501ae1fdeb04\n  modified: 2026-08-03T23:18:09.932Z\n---\n\nEric's limiting factor on deep work is environmental, not motivational. He is highly productive in short bursts once work actually starts; what breaks is the path to starting. The specific disruptor he named (2026-07-28) is the pull to share what he's working on — with no standing outlet for it, he goes and finds people, which costs him the session.\n\n**Corrected 2026-07-29.** The pull toward people is not a leak, it's the generative stage. His own observation: alone he wants company; in company the project ideas arrive. Solitude is where he refines, not where he generates. A long unbroken solo block with nothing in the hopper is waiting, not deep work.\n\n**Second brake, same day:** he holds ideas back from fear of overstepping into others' lanes, against an unstated expectation that he act unasked. He is deliberately moving off this position (\"if I don't act, ideas wither\"). Pressfield's Resistance is his frame for it.\n\n**Qualified 2026-08-03.** The permission gate is not entirely self-imposed. He acted unasked on a device another staffer was already championing (ShiftWave chair, second trial unit) and took real organizational blowback for it — careful, low-key handling bought nothing, because the claim was made by initiating, not by presenting. So the caution is warranted on ground someone has visibly attached themselves to, and unwarranted everywhere else. His same-day reaction (\"pander to the people in power, keep your head down\") is a bruise, not necessarily a settled position — don't treat it as one.\n\n**Why:** Both reframe what looks like procrastination. The first is an unmet structural need (input/ideation) improvising in real time; the second is a self-imposed permission gate that reads as professional courtesy. Neither is a discipline failure, and treating them as one produces useless advice.\n\n**How to apply:** Act as the thinking partner in-session — reflect his reasoning back, name what's interesting in it, and give him something to react to rather than only executing tasks silently. When he's ideating, don't rush to implementation; the ideation *is* the need being met, and it's the stage that feeds everything else — help him capture what surfaces. Prefer short, well-scoped blocks with a clear starting line. When he hesitates on whether something is his to do, first ask whether anyone has visibly claimed it — if not, name the permission gate rather than validating the caution; if someone has, the hesitation is a correct read and the useful move is how to approach the owner, not encouragement to proceed. Related: [[company-generates-solitude-refines]], [[waiting-for-the-ask-kills-the-idea]], [[ownership-not-merit-governs-the-reaction]], [[design-the-conditions-not-the-discipline]] in the SecondBrain vault.\n\n\n## Instructions\n\nLook for:\n- Direct contradictions (one file says X, another says not-X)\n- The same fact stated in two files, where one is more current than the other\n- Statements that name a path, tool, schedule or version that another file says has moved,\n  been retired, or been replaced\n- Advice in one file that a later file's experience overturns\n\nFor each issue found, output EXACTLY one line in this format:\nCONTRADICTION: [file1] vs [file2] - description of the conflict\nREDUNDANT: [file1] vs [file2] - what is duplicated and which looks newer\nSTALE: [file] - what it asserts and what supersedes it\n\nIf no issues found, output exactly: NO_ISSUES\n\nDo NOT output anything else - no preamble, no explanation, just the formatted lines.\n\n**Assistant:** Request timed out"}
